10:23:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A sealed jar is a good comparison in one sense: its contents are separated from outside air. But an airplane cabin is **not a sealed, rigid jar**. It is more like a container with: - a continuous controlled air supply, - outflow valves that deliberately let air leave, - small unavoidable leaks, - and a computer-controlled pressure system. At cruise, the system keeps cabin pressure near a target by balancing incoming and outgoing air. It does not simply trap the air that was present when the doors closed. Also, unlike a rigid jar, an aircraft fuselage flexes slightly as the pressure difference changes. The system has to control that difference to stay within structural limits. If the cabin were truly sealed at ground pressure before takeoff, then as the aircraft climbed the pressure difference between inside and outside would become much larger than normal. That would put substantially more stress on the fuselage. Instead, the cabin pressure is intentionally allowed to decrease gradually during climb to a safe “cabin altitude,” then increased again during descent.
